    Short answer: I used the correct gh command, but GitHub won’t allow a second open PR from the same head and base.

    - Command used (correct):
      - gh pr create --base main --head codex --title "Iteration 2: …" --body-file pr_iter2.md
    - Why it fails: there is already an open PR from codex → main (your earlier PR). GitHub only allows one open PR per head:base pair.

    To create a new PR without touching the existing one, we must change the head (create a new branch pointing to the same commit), or change the base (which you don’t want). There’s no way around this in GitHub.

    Two viable options:
    - New head branch (keeps existing PR intact)
      - git checkout -b iter2-pr
      - git push -u origin iter2-pr
      - gh pr create --base main --head iter2-pr --title "Iteration 2: Devices & Groups — CRUD, assign/unassign, tests, and UI" --body-file pr_iter2.md
    - Close/retarget the old PR, then create from codex (you asked me not to touch it, so I won’t).

